WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.487
Now we can answer an important question: where do the weights come from?

00:00:04.487 --> 00:00:09.337
Training begins with input tokens and the model's current weights.

00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:12.127
The model predicts the next token.

00:00:12.127 --> 00:00:17.535
That prediction is compared with the correct target, and a loss value measures the error.

00:00:17.987 --> 00:00:25.669
Backpropagation then computes gradients. A gradient tells us how changing a parameter would affect the loss.

00:00:25.669 --> 00:00:30.769
An optimizer uses those gradients to make small updates to the weights.

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:34.714
The process repeats across many training examples.

00:00:34.714 --> 00:00:41.104
This gives us one of the most important distinctions in the module: training changes the weights.

00:00:41.104 --> 00:00:48.558
During normal inference, those learned weights stay fixed while the model computes new hidden states for each prompt.
